/*
time complexity - O(n)
space complexity - O(n)
INTUITION
1. Use a stack to store indices of increasing bar heights.

We keep the stack in strictly increasing order of heights.
Why?
Because as long as bars keep increasing, each bar “hopes" to extend its rectangle to the right.

2. When you see a bar shorter than the stack top → POP

A shorter bar cuts off the rectangle of any taller bar before it.

So the moment you see a smaller height:

The bar at the top of the stack cannot extend any further right.

Its next smaller bar on the right = current index i.

This is when you compute area for that bar.

3. For every popped bar:

Let height = heights[popped]

The right boundary = i (current index)

The left boundary = index just below the popped one in the stack

Thus:

width = (i - left_smaller_index - 1)


If stack is empty:

width = i


This means the bar extends all the way to the beginning.

Compute area:

area = height * width


Update max.

4. Continue until stack is increasing again

Once the shorter bar is handled, push the current index into the stack.

5. Add a virtual bar of height 0 at the end

That’s why the loop goes:

for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++)


At i == n, treat height as 0 → forces all remaining bars to be popped & processed.


*/



public class Solution {
    public int largestRectangleArea(int[] heights) {
        int n = heights.length;
        int maxArea = 0;
        Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();

        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            while (!stack.isEmpty() &&
                 (i == n || heights[stack.peek()] >= heights[i])) {
                int height = heights[stack.pop()];
                int width = stack.isEmpty() ? i : i - stack.peek() - 1;
                maxArea = Math.max(maxArea, height * width);
            }
            stack.push(i);
        }
        return maxArea;
    }
}
